      <section class="story-section project-chapter-first" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="90">
        <h2>Search turns the whole site into one navigable space</h2>
        <p>When I remembered something I had written, finding its page was only part of getting back to it. The relevant passage could be well down a long collection or project page. I made Search show the matching text and open its precise location, keeping the surrounding context within reach. Visitors can use the same route into the archive without first learning how I organised it. Try a name or idea below and follow a result to its source.</p>
        <SearchPalette
          embedded
          fixed-mode="search"
          initial-query="explore"
        />
        <p>Remembering a name and remembering an idea gave Search different problems to solve. A query for HSBC supplies a precise term that appears in the text. A question about translating business requirements into software may point to the same project without sharing much of its wording. Someone encountering the work for the first time might describe it differently again. I combined lexical and semantic retrieval so both kinds of query could lead to the same source.</p>
        <p>Lexical matching scores the words shared by the query and a passage, preserving useful signals from names and technical terms. Semantic retrieval compares their embeddings, numerical representations produced by the same sentence-embedding model. Passages with related meanings can then be found even when they share little wording with the question.</p>
        <p>
          Because these methods produce scores on different scales, I combine their rankings using weighted reciprocal-rank fusion, which scores a passage by its position
          in each list. Giving lexical matches extra weight keeps precise names prominent while allowing a broadly
          phrased question to find material whose wording is quite different.
        </p>
        <div id="retrieval-before-generation" class="interactive-surface retrieval-surface" data-scroll-pop="true">
          <div class="surface-heading">
            <span>Compare word and meaning matches</span>
            <p>Try the same query across lexical and semantic retrieval and see what each one finds before the two rankings are combined.</p>
          </div>
          <RetrievalLab />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="90">
        <div class="impl-details">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="impl-details-toggle"
            :aria-expanded="searchDetailsOpen.toString()"
            aria-controls="search-details-panel"
            @click="searchDetailsOpen = !searchDetailsOpen"
          >
            <span>How Search works</span>
            <span class="impl-details-caret" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <div id="search-details-panel" v-show="searchDetailsOpen" class="impl-details-panel">
            <p>The published material changes when I update the site, so its embeddings can be prepared during the build and reused for subsequent searches. I use all-MiniLM-L6-v2, a compact sentence-embedding model, to represent each passage as a 384-dimensional vector. Each passage retains its original page and location. Quantising the vectors into 8-bit integers, together with the scale needed for comparison, reduces the data shipped with the site.</p>
            <p>
              At search time, the browser uses the same model to embed the query. Cosine similarity measures how closely
              that query aligns with each stored passage embedding. Lexical ranking runs alongside it with a
              TF-IDF-like score that gives more weight to informative words across the site. With passage embeddings
              already available, the browser can embed the query and run retrieval locally without sending it to a search API or incurring a per-query fee.
            </p>
            <p>A ranking will always put something first, including when the archive has little to offer a query. I tested realistic searches alongside deliberately unrelated ones to see where a useful similarity threshold might sit. In that small test set, unrelated examples stayed at or below about 0.22, most genuine matches scored above 0.45, and a few useful matches fell near 0.26. I chose a floor of 0.24 to retain those weaker relevant matches while filtering out clearly unrelated results.</p>
            <p>I also tried e5-small, whose scores occupied a narrower range. That made an absolute threshold less useful, and its recall did not improve enough to justify the trade-off. I kept MiniLM because the clearer separation in these tests gave me a more useful basis for deciding which semantic matches to show.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="90">
        <h2>Seeing the archive as a space</h2>
