        <p>Once the passages had embeddings, I could compare them with one another as well as with a query. That suggested another way to explore the collection. Material filed under research, work, or personal writing could sit together because of what it discussed. I used those relationships to make a map of the archive, giving visitors a way to look around neighbouring ideas even before choosing a particular question.</p>
        <p>
          I use UMAP to project the high-dimensional passage embeddings into two or three dimensions so they can be
          drawn on a screen. Both projections are prepared when the site is built, with a fixed seed that keeps the
          arrangement stable until the underlying material changes.
        </p>
        <div id="shared-memory" class="interactive-surface embedding-surface" data-scroll-pop="true">
          <EmbeddingMap initial-view-mode="3d" two-finger-touch />
